WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.280
In George Orwell's 1984,

00:00:02.280 --> 00:00:04.260
the government is trying
to force Britain,

00:00:04.260 --> 00:00:07.320
or rather, "Airstrip One",
to use Newspeak:

00:00:07.320 --> 00:00:09.960
a constructed language that
would end "thought-crime".

00:00:09.960 --> 00:00:12.780
The goal, so the story goes,
is to make it impossible

00:00:12.780 --> 00:00:15.480
to form ideas that could
challenge the ruling Party

00:00:15.480 --> 00:00:18.780
by taking away the words
to think them with.

00:00:19.620 --> 00:00:22.740
Linguists often cringe when
they hear people talk about

00:00:22.740 --> 00:00:25.860
the idea that the language you speak
determines how you can think.

00:00:25.860 --> 00:00:29.160
Because it's… well, it's
not really a thing.

00:00:29.160 --> 00:00:32.280
It's been called the
"Sapir-Whorf Hypothesis",

00:00:32.280 --> 00:00:34.500
because it's associated with
Benjamin Lee Whorf,

00:00:34.500 --> 00:00:37.920
an early 20th century linguist,
and his mentor, Edward Sapir.

00:00:37.920 --> 00:00:41.340
They weren't the first, the last,
or the loudest linguists to

00:00:41.340 --> 00:00:44.880
ask the question, and they never
wrote anything even close

00:00:44.880 --> 00:00:46.380
to a hypothesis about it together.

00:00:46.380 --> 00:00:49.200
They wondered how language
could impact thought,

00:00:49.200 --> 00:00:51.480
and vice versa,
but no more than that.

00:00:51.480 --> 00:00:52.980
The concept is known as

00:00:52.980 --> 00:00:55.020
linguistic determinism
in its strong form,

00:00:55.020 --> 00:00:57.720
and linguistic relativism
in its weak form.

00:00:57.720 --> 00:01:01.620
Determinism, the "strong" version,
is a popular trope in fiction,

00:01:01.620 --> 00:01:02.880
particularly science fiction.

00:01:04.380 --> 00:01:05.580
Which is where it belongs!

00:01:05.580 --> 00:01:09.180
The film Arrival made it a major
plot point and actually name-checked it.

00:01:09.180 --> 00:01:12.360
"If you immerse yourself into
a foreign language, that you

00:01:12.360 --> 00:01:14.340
"can actually rewire your brain?"

00:01:14.340 --> 00:01:17.580
"The Sapir-Whorf Hypothesis."
"Hmm."

00:01:17.580 --> 00:01:20.760
The short story it's based on is much better.

00:01:20.760 --> 00:01:25.020
Linguistic determinism is not taken very
seriously by the linguistics community,

00:01:25.020 --> 00:01:26.760
as it's easily disprovable.

00:01:26.760 --> 00:01:30.720
Either by attending a Philosophy 101 course,
or just thinking about the phrase,

00:01:30.720 --> 00:01:34.800
"I wish I could find the words
to explain how I feel."

00:01:34.800 --> 00:01:38.640
But the weak form, linguistic relativism,
is much more broadly accepted.

00:01:38.640 --> 00:01:42.600
It is a difficult and messy thing
to test for and prove, though.

00:01:42.600 --> 00:01:45.120
You can find a lot of people
claiming that the Russian language

00:01:45.120 --> 00:01:46.500
has no word for "privacy",

00:01:46.500 --> 00:01:49.980
because the concept of 'privacy' isn't
part of traditional Russian culture?

00:01:49.980 --> 00:01:51.360
That might be true,

00:01:51.360 --> 00:01:52.740
I'm not Russian,
I couldn't tell you,

00:01:52.740 --> 00:01:55.620
but that wouldn't be language
limiting the way you think,

00:01:55.620 --> 00:01:58.560
that would be language
reflecting the way you think.

00:01:58.560 --> 00:02:01.440
There have been studies
that show things like:

00:02:01.440 --> 00:02:04.620
German speakers, whose language
says that 'key' is 'masculine',

00:02:04.620 --> 00:02:07.860
are more likely to describe a key
as 'jagged', 'rough', and 'hard',

00:02:07.860 --> 00:02:10.380
whereas Spanish speakers,
where 'key' is 'feminine',

00:02:10.380 --> 00:02:13.320
would be more likely to use
'golden' or 'intricate'.

00:02:13.320 --> 00:02:16.200
I've referred to that
exact study here in the past.

00:02:16.200 --> 00:02:19.800
But when other researchers tried
to replicate that study,

00:02:19.800 --> 00:02:21.360
they didn't find the same result,

00:02:21.360 --> 00:02:23.100
which is something that
keeps happening

00:02:23.100 --> 00:02:25.020
across psychology
and other fields.

00:02:25.020 --> 00:02:27.900
And besides, it's very difficult
to tease apart

00:02:27.900 --> 00:02:30.840
the impacts of your culture and
the impacts of your language.

00:02:30.840 --> 00:02:33.840
Even if that result is true,
which it's probably not…

00:02:33.840 --> 00:02:37.440
is the gendered language the cause,
or the effect?

00:02:38.100 --> 00:02:40.560
There are some clear impacts on how

00:02:40.560 --> 00:02:43.080
different languages, not cultures,
affect the brain.

00:02:43.080 --> 00:02:46.020
For example, our hearing tunes
into the phonology, the sounds,

00:02:46.020 --> 00:02:47.100
of the languages we speak.

00:02:47.100 --> 00:02:50.160
In Hindi, for example,
[tʰal] and [tal]

00:02:50.160 --> 00:02:52.200
are two different words,
platter and rhythm,

00:02:52.200 --> 00:02:55.680
because [tʰ] and [t] are considered
different sounds,

00:02:55.680 --> 00:02:58.200
based on the aspiration,
that air that comes out.

00:02:58.200 --> 00:03:01.320
That's not a cultural value,
that's not ideology,

00:03:01.320 --> 00:03:03.480
it's not passed down
deliberately to children.

00:03:04.200 --> 00:03:05.160
That's just language.

00:03:05.160 --> 00:03:07.380
If you speak a language
with that distinction,

00:03:07.380 --> 00:03:10.200
your brain will hear it
automatically and unconsciously.

00:03:10.200 --> 00:03:13.020
And if you don't, you have to try
to actively listen for it.

00:03:13.020 --> 00:03:16.440
That's a pretty clear way that
languages impact thought.

00:03:16.440 --> 00:03:19.740
But that doesn't feel close enough
to the science fiction, does it?

00:03:19.740 --> 00:03:23.040
It's not like it's changing your
understanding of the world.

00:03:23.040 --> 00:03:25.980
Science fiction wants the
language you speak

00:03:25.980 --> 00:03:28.020
to affect the categories
in your head,

00:03:28.020 --> 00:03:29.640
the way you understand the world,

00:03:29.640 --> 00:03:33.840
particularly for abstract concepts,
rather than actual physical things.

00:03:33.840 --> 00:03:36.660
So what about emotions?

00:03:36.660 --> 00:03:38.760
Emotions do get categorized

00:03:38.760 --> 00:03:41.400
in different ways by different humans
all over the world.

00:03:41.400 --> 00:03:44.220
In English, we can use the same word
for two different feelings.

00:03:44.220 --> 00:03:45.900
If someone says "I'm sad",

00:03:45.900 --> 00:03:48.240
do they mean
"crying in the corner"

00:03:48.240 --> 00:03:51.180
or "depressed, tired,
and staying in bed"?

00:03:51.180 --> 00:03:52.920
Those are both sad

00:03:52.920 --> 00:03:55.680
in the same way that the sky
and the ocean are both blue.

00:03:55.680 --> 00:03:58.260
But even if a child
only knows the word "sad",

00:03:58.260 --> 00:04:02.034
they've not heard of "despondent'
or 'listless' or 'morose' or 'ennui',

00:04:02.034 --> 00:04:03.600
well, the underlying emotions
are still different,

00:04:03.600 --> 00:04:05.280
they can still tell whether someone's

00:04:05.280 --> 00:04:08.760
grieving the devastating loss of
their cartoonishly large lollipop

00:04:08.760 --> 00:04:11.340
or whether they're just tired
and generally down.

00:04:11.340 --> 00:04:14.820
Having the different words is really
helpful for expressing yourself,

00:04:14.820 --> 00:04:18.420
but they don't determine
your ability to feel emotions.

00:04:18.420 --> 00:04:21.600
And Ancient Greek had a
lot of words for love:

00:04:21.600 --> 00:04:25.080
in English those are all
basically translated as 'love'

00:04:25.080 --> 00:04:26.460
with a lot of extra notes,

00:04:26.460 --> 00:04:28.860
but even though we only have
one basic word

00:04:28.860 --> 00:04:31.140
to explain all those
feelings and emotions,

00:04:31.140 --> 00:04:32.820
we still experience them,

00:04:32.820 --> 00:04:35.610
and we can communicate them
without knowing Ancient Greek.

00:04:35.610 --> 00:04:37.200
It just takes a few more words.

00:04:37.200 --> 00:04:40.080
Until recently, English did not
have a word for

00:04:40.080 --> 00:04:42.420
"feeling joy at
someone else's misfortune",

00:04:42.420 --> 00:04:44.580
but we still definitely
experienced it

00:04:44.580 --> 00:04:47.160
before we borrowed
"schadenfreude" from German.

00:04:47.820 --> 00:04:50.700
So the simple answer
to the question is: no.

00:04:50.700 --> 00:04:53.220
Not really. It might change how
you listen to sounds,

00:04:53.220 --> 00:04:55.080
it might make some things
easier to explain,

00:04:55.080 --> 00:04:57.420
but the idea that
you can stop people

00:04:57.420 --> 00:05:00.420
bringing down the government
by changing their language is,

00:05:01.200 --> 00:05:02.940
thankfully, just fiction.

00:05:03.720 --> 00:05:06.780
And if you want even more linguistics,
my co-author Gretchen McCulloch

00:05:06.780 --> 00:05:09.000
has a podcast called
"Lingthusiasm".

00:05:09.000 --> 00:05:10.980
The link is on screen
and in the description.

